  "output": "I read through both layers. Bottom line: this codebase is Phase 1 scaffolding — most of the pipeline (`blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `graph.py`, `main.py:execute_run`) is `raise NotImplementedError`, and the web app has exactly one real data-driven screen (`/sources`, a single `select()` with no joins). So most classic findings don't apply yet — there's nothing to have an N+1 pattern *in*. What's useful right now is flagging where the current code already sets a trap, and where AGENTS.md's own architecture calls out risks that need to be honored when Phase 2–4 land.\n\n## N+1 queries\n**Not present yet.** The only query is `listSources()` (`web/lib/server/sources.ts:19`) — one `select().from(sources)`.\n\nReal risk to preempt: `countSourcesBySide()` (same file, line 23-30) calls `listSources()` again and filters in JS instead of reusing the already-fetched array or pushing the count into SQL. Today it's cheap (one table, no caller does both in the same request), but if a page ever calls both `listSources()` and `countSourcesBySide()` on the same render, that's two round trips for data you already have:\n\n```ts\n// current: two separate queries if both are called\nexport async function listSources(): Promise<Source[]> { ... }\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources(); // re-queries\n  ...\n}\n\n// fix when a page needs both: pass the already-fetched rows in\nexport function countSourcesBySide(all: Source[]) {\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThe real N+1 exposure is Phase 4's conflict queue and audit trail — `conflicts` → `matches` → `entities` is a natural place to fetch entities per-conflict in a loop. When you build `lib/server/conflicts.ts`, join in one Drizzle query rather than fetching a conflict list then looping to fetch each entity.\n\n## Unnecessary React re-renders\nAlmost the whole app is Server Components by design (AGENTS.md's own rule), which sidesteps this class of bug entirely. The one Client Component that exists, `NavRail` (`web/components/console/nav-rail.tsx`), is fine as-is: `usePathname()` + `useUser()` only re-render the rail itself, not the page content, since it's a leaf.\n\nForward-looking risk: the conflict queue (Phase 4) is specified as keyboard-navigable (`j`/`k`/`a`/`e`/`o`) over \"a queue of 400\" rows — that's a client component by necessity, and a naive implementation (row selection state at the list level, inline handlers recreated per row) will re-render all 400 rows on every keypress. When you build it:\n\n```tsx\n// avoid: selection state + handlers in the parent re-render every row\nfunction ConflictQueue({ conflicts }) {\n  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(0);\n  return conflicts.map((c, i) => (\n    <ConflictDiffRow key={c.id} conflict={c} isSelected={i === selected} onSelect={() => setSelected(i)} />\n  ));\n}\n\n// better: memoize rows, key selection by id not index, avoid inline closures\nconst ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ conflict, isSelected }: Props) { ... });\n```\n\n## Caching opportunities\nTwo real ones, both already anticipated in the codebase/AGENTS.md but worth stating explicitly:\n\n1. **`web/lib/db/index.ts`** already does the right thing — caches the Postgres connection on `globalThis` across HMR reloads (line 22-25). Good pattern, nothing to fix.\n2. **Anthropic prompt caching on the ruleset block** — AGENTS.md calls this out as \"the single largest cost lever\" for the resolution agent, but `agent/graph.py` doesn't call the Anthropic API yet (`resolve()` is a stub). Flag it now so it's not forgotten in Phase 3: when `propose` is implemented, the ruleset (identical across every conflict in a run) needs `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` on that content block, not on the per-conflict diff.\n3. TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling (per AGENTS.md) but no run-status page exists yet — when built, set a `staleTime`/`refetchInterval` that stops polling once `status` is terminal (`complete`/`failed`), or you'll poll forever on a page left open.\n\n## Memory leaks\nNothing currently leaks — there's no `setInterval`, `addEventListener`, WebSocket, or subscription in the codebase. The one thing to watch: the future run-status polling hook (TanStack Query `refetchInterval`) needs to actually stop on unmount and on terminal status — that's the standard leak vector for this exact pattern (interval keeps firing after navigating away, or after the run finishes, hammering `GET /runs/:id/status`).\n\n## Redundant computations\n`blocking.py` already documents the one that matters most: candidate generation must stay O(n·k) via exact-key-first + FAISS top-k, \"never compare all pairs\" (line 3-4 comment) — that's a naive O(n²) matcher, and the module correctly plans to avoid it. Nothing to fix; just flag it as the thing to verify once `block()` is actually implemented (run it against the 500+ account synthetic dataset and confirm it doesn't degrade to pairwise).\n\n`SourceTable` (`web/components/sources/source-table.tsx`) does `source.config.objects.join(\", \")` inline in JSX on every render — trivial at current scale (a handful of sources), not worth memoizing.\n\n## Summary\nGiven the actual state of the repo, there's nothing broken to fix today. The one concrete, do-now item is #1 above (`countSourcesBySide` re-querying). Everything else is \"when you build Phase 2–4, here's where this class of bug will show up\" — worth keeping in mind but not actionable yet since the functions are stubs.\n",
